     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "In seventeen ninety-two, a single paper dollar was a physical receipt for twenty-four point seven five grains of pure gold. History books teach that paper money lost its gold backing because the mines simply ran dry. But governments printed far more paper receipts than the physical metal sitting locked inside their vaults. This mounting imbalance forced authorities to break the metallic promise entirely. In nineteen thirty-three, President Franklin Roosevelt made holding gold coins illegal for private citizens. Then, on August fifteen, nineteen seventy-one, President Richard Nixon officially suspended convertibility. Paper money finally stood alone, permanently untethered from any physical metal."
    }
